Q: What is the Euler system for?
A: The Euler system is for solving difficult optimization problems involving programs. Specifically, it aims to find an input \( x \) to a function \( P \) (written in a standard C-like language) such that the output \( P(x) \) is minimized with respect to an appropriate distance measure on the space of outputs of \( P \). This is particularly relevant for problems in software engineering that can be framed as optimization questions.
Sources: DBLP conf cav ChaudhuriS12 (2012); DBLP conf icml TavaresK0DS21 (2021)
