Q: What is adaptive concretization?
A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique that enables efficient parallelization of challenging synthesis problems. It dynamically identifies which unknowns in a synthesis problem are best suited for explicit search and which are better suited for symbolic search through constraint solving. The main idea is to partially concretize a randomly chosen subset of the unknowns and use a combination of exponential hill climbing and binary search to optimize the size of this subset. This approach allows for a highly parallel implementation, improving the speed of the search process. Adaptive concretization has been shown to be effective, outperforming traditional synthesis methods in many cases.
Sources: DBLP conf cav JeonQSF15 (2015); DBLP journals fmsd JeonQSF17 (2017)
