Q: What is adaptive concretization?
A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique that enables efficient parallelization of challenging synthesis problems. It involves partially concretizing a randomly chosen, but likely highly influential, subset of the unknowns to be synthesized. The key observation is that some unknowns are better suited for explicit search while others are better for symbolic search through constraint solving. The main idea is to dynamically identify which unknowns are best suited to which kind of search and to parallelize the explicit search on those unknowns.

As stated in the documents, "adaptive concretization uses an online search process to find the optimal size of the concretized subset using a combination of exponential hill climbing and binary search." This approach allows for a highly parallel implementation, which can significantly speed up the search process. The effectiveness of adaptive concretization has been evaluated, showing that it can outperform traditional methods in many cases and has good parallel scalability. 

References:
- [paper: Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis | year: 2015]
- [paper: An empirical study of adaptive concretization for parallel program synthesis | year: 2017]
