Q: What is JSKETCH and what problem does it address?
A: JSKETCH is a tool that brings sketch-based synthesis to Java, allowing developers to synthesize software starting from a partial program, also known as a sketch or template. The input to JSKETCH is a partial Java program that may include "holes," which are unknown constants, as well as expression generators and class generators. JSKETCH translates the synthesis problem into a SKETCH problem, which is complex due to the non-object-oriented nature of SKETCH. Ultimately, JSKETCH synthesizes an executable Java program by interpreting the results of the SKETCH synthesis.

The problem JSKETCH addresses is the need for synthesizing complete Java programs from incomplete specifications, enabling developers to fill in the gaps in their code effectively. As stated in the paper, "JSKETCH then synthesizes an executable Java program by interpreting the synthesis results." 

This information is derived from the paper titled "JSKETCH: Sketching for Java" published in 2015.
