Q: What is adaptive concretization?
A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique designed to enhance the efficiency of parallel program synthesis. It involves "partially concretizing a randomly chosen, but likely highly influential, subset of the unknowns to be synthesized." The method employs an online search process that combines "exponential hill climbing and binary search," using a statistical test to determine the optimal size of the concretized subset. This approach allows for the dynamic identification of which unknowns are best suited for explicit search versus symbolic search, thereby enabling effective parallelization of the synthesis process.

The technique was introduced in the paper titled "Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis" (2015) and further evaluated in an empirical study published in 2017. The studies found that adaptive concretization is "very effective, outperforming Sketch in many cases" and demonstrates good parallel scalability.
Sources: Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015); An empirical study of adaptive concretization for parallel program synthesis (2017)
