Counterexample-Guided Inductive Synthesis (CEGIS) is used to find the program holes by iteratively refining the candidate solutions. It operates by first generating a solution that works for a small set of inputs and then checking if this solution is correct for all inputs. If the solution is not correct, a counterexample is generated, which is then used to refine the next candidate solution. This process continues until a satisfactory solution is found or it is determined that no such solution exists (Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015)).
Sources: JSKETCH: Sketching for Java (2015); The Sketching Approach to Program Synthesis (2009); Automated Feedback Generation for Introductory Programming Assignments (2012); CAV 2021 proceedings volume (2021); Synthesizing Entity Matching Rules by Examples (2017); Synthesis of Recursive ADT Transformations from Reusable Templates (2015); SyGuS-Comp 2017: Results and Analysis (2017); Automatic Generation of Formula Simplifiers based on Conditional Rewrite Rules (2016); Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015); SPT: Storyboard Programming Tool (2012)
