Q: What is adaptive concretization?
A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique that enhances the efficiency of solving challenging synthesis problems by combining symbolic and explicit search strategies. The main idea is to dynamically identify which unknowns in a synthesis problem are best suited for explicit search and which are better for symbolic search through constraint solving. This allows for the parallelization of the explicit search on those unknowns that are more suitable for that style of search. 

As described in the documents, "adaptive concretization uses an online search process to find the optimal size of the concretized subset" and employs a combination of "exponential hill climbing and binary search" to optimize the search process. The technique has been shown to be effective and scalable, outperforming traditional methods in many cases (Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015)).
Sources: An empirical study of adaptive concretization for parallel program synthesis (2017); Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015)
