A: The 2016 paper titled "sk_p: a neural program corrector for MOOCs" presents a technique for automatic program correction in MOOCs, focusing on fixing both syntactic and semantic errors. It utilizes a seq2seq neural network model to generate candidate programs from a distribution of likely corrections and checks these candidates against a test suite. The paper highlights that "in MOOCs many programs share similar code fragments," which allows the model to effectively recover these fragments. The approach is noted for correcting 29% of incorrect submissions and outperforming state-of-the-art methods that require manual, problem-specific correction strategies (sk_p: a neural program corrector for MOOCs (2016)).

