Both versions agree on the core functionality of the sk_p system, which is to correct programming assignments in MOOCs using a data-driven approach. However, the journal version provides a more detailed analysis of the model's capabilities, particularly regarding its syntactic nature and the novelty of the corrections it can generate. The journal version also includes a comparison of the accuracy of sk_p against traditional autograders, noting that sk_p achieves an average accuracy of 35% on specific assignments, while traditional methods may perform better on certain tasks due to their manual tuning (sk_p: a neural program corrector for MOOCs (2016)).

In summary, while both papers discuss the same neural program corrector, the journal version offers a deeper exploration of its mechanisms and performance metrics, particularly in terms of syntactic handling and the generation of fresh corrections.
