Q: What is the main idea of the sketching approach to program synthesis?
A: The main idea of the sketching approach to program synthesis is to allow programmers to express their high-level insights about a problem by writing a sketch, which is a partial program that encodes the structure of a solution while leaving its low-level details unspecified. The programmer provides the synthesizer with either a reference implementation or a set of test routines that the synthesized code must pass. The Sketch synthesis engine then derives the missing details in the sketch to produce a working implementation that satisfies the correctness criteria established by the programmer. This approach combines the programmer's insights with the synthesizer's ability to reason exhaustively about low-level details, enabling the implementation of complex algorithms. As stated in the document, "the synthesizer's ability to reason exhaustively about all the low-level details of the problem" is a key aspect of this method (The Sketching Approach to Program Synthesis (2009)).
