Q: What is adaptive concretization?
A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique that combines symbolic and explicit search strategies to efficiently solve synthesis problems. The main idea is to dynamically identify which unknowns in a synthesis problem are best suited for explicit search and which are better for symbolic search through constraint solving. This allows for the parallelization of the explicit search on those unknowns that are more suitable for that style of search.

In the context of adaptive concretization, the algorithm works by "partially concretizing a randomly chosen, but likely highly influential, subset of the unknowns to be synthesized." It employs an online search process to determine the optimal size of this concretized subset, using methods like exponential hill climbing and binary search. The approach has been shown to be effective, outperforming traditional methods in many cases and demonstrating good parallel scalability (Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015)).
Sources: An empirical study of adaptive concretization for parallel program synthesis (2017); Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015)
