Q: What is adaptive concretization?
A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique that aims to efficiently parallelize challenging synthesis problems. It involves dynamically identifying which unknowns in a synthesis problem are best suited for explicit search and which are better for symbolic search through constraint solving. The technique partially concretizes a randomly chosen, likely influential subset of the unknowns to be synthesized. 

According to the documents, "the main idea behind adaptive concretization is to dynamically identify which unknowns are best suited to which kind of search, and to parallelize the explicit search on those unknowns for which that style of search is more suitable" (An empirical study of adaptive concretization for parallel program synthesis (2017)). The algorithm employs an online search process to find the optimal size of the concretized subset and is designed for high parallel implementation, which speeds up the search process (Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015)).
