A: CEGIS stands for "Counterexample Guided Inductive Synthesis." It is a synthesis algorithm that guarantees total correctness on a set of examples. CEGIS consists of two adversarial sub-routines: a synthesizer that produces a candidate program and a checker that generates adversarial counterexamples to validate the candidate. The process continues until the checker fails to produce a counterexample, indicating that the synthesized program is correct for the given examples (Selecting Representative Examples for Program Synthesis (2018)).
