A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique that enhances the efficiency of solving challenging synthesis problems by combining symbolic and explicit search strategies. It dynamically identifies which unknowns in a synthesis problem are best suited for explicit search and which are better for symbolic search through constraint solving. The process involves partially concretizing a randomly chosen subset of the unknowns, allowing for a more effective search strategy. The algorithm employs an online search to optimize the size of the concretized subset, utilizing techniques like exponential hill climbing and binary search to determine the most effective degree of concretization.

The key idea is to "dynamically identify which unknowns are best suited to which kind of search, and to parallelize the explicit search on those unknowns for which that style of search is more suitable" (An empirical study of adaptive concretization for parallel program synthesis (2017)). This approach has shown to be effective in improving performance and scalability in parallel implementations of program synthesis tasks (Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015)).
Sources: An empirical study of adaptive concretization for parallel program synthesis (2017); Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis (2015)
