Q: What does the LINC paper do with logical reasoning?
A: The LINC paper presents a neurosymbolic approach to logical reasoning, termed "Logical Inference via Neurosymbolic Computation." In this framework, the process involves two main steps: first, a language model (LLM) translates natural language premises and conclusions into first-order logic (FOL) expressions. Then, these expressions are processed by an external theorem prover that determines the truth value of the conclusion based on the formalized premises. The paper notes that "a symbolic FOL theorem prover algorithmically determines the truth value of the conclusion given the formalized premises" and also incorporates a majority voting step to enhance performance (LINC: A Neurosymbolic Approach for Logical Reasoning by Combining Language Models with First-Order Logic Provers (2023)).
Sources: LINC: A Neurosymbolic Approach for Logical Reasoning by Combining Language Models with First-Order Logic Provers (2023); Position: Future Research and Challenges Remain Towards AI for Software Engineering (2025); Program Synthesis from Polymorphic Refinement Types (2016)
