Q: How do verifiers act as oracles in CEGIS?
A: In the context of CEGIS (Counterexample-Guided Inductive Synthesis), verifiers act as oracles by checking candidate programs against a set of examples and providing feedback. Specifically, the checker takes in a candidate program \( s \) and produces an adversarial counterexample \( ce \) that invalidates \( s \). This counterexample is then added to the subset of examples, prompting the synthesizer to improve the candidate program. The process continues until the checker fails to produce an adversarial example, indicating that the synthesis has successfully terminated.

The paper describes this mechanism, stating: "The checker takes in this candidate program s and produces an adversarial counter example ce D \D which invalidates s." This indicates that the checker serves the role of an oracle by providing critical information that guides the synthesis process (Selecting Representative Examples for Program Synthesis (2018)).
Sources: Selecting Representative Examples for Program Synthesis (2018); MIMEQA: Towards Socially-Intelligent Nonverbal Foundation Models (2025); CAV 2021 proceedings volume
