Q: What is the main idea of the sketching approach to program synthesis?
A: The main idea of the sketching approach to program synthesis is to allow programmers to express their high-level insights about a problem by writing a sketch, which is a partial program that encodes the structure of a solution while leaving its low-level details unspecified. The programmer provides the synthesizer with either a reference implementation or a set of test routines that the synthesized code must pass. The Sketch synthesis engine then derives the missing details in the sketch to produce a working implementation that satisfies the correctness criteria established by the programmer. This approach combines the programmer's insights with the synthesizer's ability to reason about low-level details, enabling the implementation of complex algorithms (The Sketching Approach to Program Synthesis (2009)).
Sources: The Sketching Approach to Program Synthesis (2009); Synthesis of Recursive ADT Transformations from Reusable Templates (2015); Learning to Infer Program Sketches (2019); Unsupervised Learning by Program Synthesis (2015); JSKETCH: Sketching for Java (2015)
