A: The main idea of the sketching approach to program synthesis is to enable programmers to express their high-level insights about a problem by writing a "sketch," which is a partial program that outlines the structure of a solution while leaving the low-level implementation details unspecified. This approach allows programmers to focus on the essential aspects of the problem without getting bogged down by the complexities of implementation.

In this method, programmers provide the synthesizer with either a reference implementation or a set of test routines that the synthesized code must pass. The synthesis engine then derives the missing details in the sketch to produce a working implementation that meets the correctness criteria established by the programmer. This combination of programmer insights and the synthesizer's ability to reason about low-level details facilitates the implementation of complex algorithms.

As described in the papers, "the Sketch synthesis engine is able to derive the missing details in the sketch to produce a working implementation that satisfies the correctness criteria established by the programmer" (The Sketching Approach to Program Synthesis (2009)). This highlights the collaborative nature of the sketching approach, where the programmer's high-level design is complemented by the synthesizer's exhaustive reasoning capabilities.
Sources: The Sketching Approach to Program Synthesis (2009); Synthesis of Recursive ADT Transformations from Reusable Templates (2015); Learning to Infer Program Sketches (2019); Unsupervised Learning by Program Synthesis (2015); JSKETCH: Sketching for Java (2015)
