Q: What is the Euler system for?
A: The Euler system is designed for solving difficult optimization problems involving programs. Specifically, it aims to find an input \( x \) to a function \( P \) (written in a standard C-like language) such that the output \( P(x) \) is minimized with respect to an appropriate distance measure on the space of outputs of \( P \). This is particularly useful for problems in software engineering that can be framed as optimization questions. As stated in the paper, "Euler is a system for solving unconstrained optimization problems" (Euler: A System for Numerical Optimization of Programs (2012)).
Sources: Euler: A System for Numerical Optimization of Programs (2012); A Language for Counterfactual Generative Models (2021)
