Q: What does the AES standard specify?
A: The AES standard specifies the Rijndael algorithm, which is a symmetric block cipher that can process data blocks of 128 bits, using cipher keys with lengths of 128, 192, and 256 bits. The standard outlines that "the AES algorithm is capable of using cryptographic keys of 128, 192, and 256 bits to encrypt and decrypt data in blocks of 128 bits" (Federal Information Processing Standards Publication 197: Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) (2001)). It also details the number of rounds performed during the execution of the algorithm, which depends on the key size: "Nr = 10 when Nk = 4, Nr = 12 when Nk = 6, and Nr = 14 when Nk = 8" (Federal Information Processing Standards Publication 197: Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) (2001)).
Sources: Federal Information Processing Standards Publication 197: Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) (2001); Liquid Information Flow Control (2020); DBLP journals corr GopalakrishnanH17 (2017)
